 3. How I use AI: Airbnb CTO Ahmad Al-Dahle 
 
 By Madison Mills 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Axios Visuals. Photo: Airbnb/Celeste Sloman

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Airbnb CTO Ahmad Al-Dahle is both a person and a "skill" — an agentic tool he uses that's based on him, trained on his feedback and perspective so he can bounce ideas off of it any time. 

 Why it matters: Al-Dahle, who previously led Meta's generative AI efforts, is embracing AI agents in his own work, but he's cautious on broader AI agent access for customers. 

 Even the most bullish AI users at companies are grappling with AI safety questions. What follows is an edited version of our conversation: 

 Axios: Take me through a day in your life of AI usage. 

 Al-Dahle: "I own a lot of different things, so part of what I have to do is broadly sweep all of the information that I need in order to be informed of what's happening across the company." Autonomous agents "scan all of my workplace tools and communication channels" like Slack and email, providing a read on various areas of the company on "almost an hourly basis if I choose." "I don't always trust exactly what it is, but it gives me enough signal to actually dive deeper." Axios: What's one AI use case you can't live without? 

 Someone at Airbnb built a tool Al-Dahle describes as "the CTO skill," trained on his feedback. "It's not bad, actually ... it has a kind of perspective I might have," he said, adding it's a helpful brainstorming tool. Keep reading .

 
 
